": 1, "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"title": "", "id": 1, "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"coverimg": "engenn10le08ob08re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "engenn10le08ob08re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "engenn10le08ob08re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": ""}, {"time": "100", "image": "engenn10le08ob08re2img01.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "Ah, the city of Babylon and its amazing gardens! The city, ruled by Nebuchadnezzar II, must have been quite a sight to those travelers who arrived there. Historians wrote about these \"hanging\" gardens, which were planted on a series of terraces supported by columns by ancient Babylonians. Some believe that the gardens were built by the king in about 600 BC in an area that is now southern Iraq.\n</br>In this period, Babylon was at the height of its power and influence. King Nebuchadnezzar is known to have ordered the construction of a vast array of buildings, including temples, and palaces to make it more beautiful. He also commissioned streets for the city and walls to surround it."}, {"time": "51500", "image": "engenn10le08ob08re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "Tradition has it that the gardens were created by the king to console his wife, who missed her homeland. Although Babylon rarely received rain, the gardens were lush and provided an oasis in the desert landscape. Even though there were no modern engines or water pumps at such an early date, the gardens were constantly irrigated with the water from the Euphrates River. It must have been an astonishing sight!"}, {"time": "82250", "image": "engenn10le08ob08re2img02.jpg", "id": 3, "text": "Despite being one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World, the Colossus of Rhodes is often overlooked. The immense statue, standing almost 100 feet high, was one of the tallest erected in ancient times. Made of bronze, the statue of the sun god Helios was erected by the citizens near the harbor of their island in about 280 BC This colossus was built as a celebration of freedom. Although the Colossus collapsed during an earthquake in 225 BC, the pieces lay untouched where they had fallen for hundreds of years.\nThen, in the middle of the seventh century, the metal fragments were gathered by invading Arab tribes and were carried off. Some have noted that it took as many as 900 camels to carry the pieces away. In spite of its destruction, historians and tourists alike still admire this majestic work of art."}]}}], "_data": [], "type": "audio", "template_override": 1}, {"title": "", "id": 2, "contents": [{"type": "video", "value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "", "id": 0, "text": "Great Pyramid of Giza\n</span><ul><li>Built: About 2600 BC.\n</li><li>Massive tombs of Egyptian pharaohs\n</li><li>Oldest of the Seven Wonders of The World\n</li><li>Is the oldest and largest of the three pyramids in the Giza pyramid complex\n</li><li>Borders what is now El Giza, Egypt\n</li><li>Consists of an estimated 2.3 million blocks\n</li><li>Was the tallest man-made structure in the world for more than 3,800 years.\n</li><li>The pyramids are the only ancient wonders still standing today.\n</li></ul>"}], "video_src": "engenn10le08ob08re3vid01"}}], "_data": [], "type": "video", "template_override": 1}], "id": 0}]}